---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Death Guard Beginner's Guide — Warhammer 40k"
  description="New to the Death Guard? Learn Nurgle's plague lore, how the unkillable army plays, how to paint rust and slime, and what to buy first."
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 border-b border-[#2a2a35]">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <a href="/factions" class="text-sm text-[#4a4855] hover:text-[#8a8590] transition-colors mb-6 inline-block">
        ← All Factions
      </a>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-6">
        <div class="text-6xl">🦠</div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[#c9a84c] text-sm font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-2 font-display">Faction Guide</p>
          <h1 class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">Death Guard</h1>
          <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Rotting. Bloated. Absolutely impossible to kill. 
            The Death Guard are Chaos Space Marines remade by Nurgle, 
            the god of plague — diseased beyond all recognition 
            but stronger for every wound they refuse to die from. 
            The most durable army in 40k, and one of the most fun to paint.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick stats -->
      <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Playstyle</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm">Slow Juggernaut</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Painting</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-green-400">Easy + Fun ✓</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Rules</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-yellow-400">Medium</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 font-display">Starter Cost</p>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm">~$160–$230</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Lore -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">The plague that made them</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg">
        <p>
          During the Horus Heresy, the Death Guard's fleet made a catastrophic error 
          and became trapped in the Warp — adrift in the space between reality, 
          becalmed without drives, with no way out. 
          Days became months. Months became something worse. 
          A plague began spreading through the fleet — 
          one so virulent that nothing slowed it, nothing stopped it. 
          Plague Marines in full power armor rotted alive inside their suits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Their Primarch, <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Mortarion</strong>, 
          had survived the most toxic world in the Imperium through sheer will. 
          He had never begged. He had never broken. 
          When his Legion was dying around him and he had no other options, 
          he did something he had never done before: 
          he called out into the Warp for salvation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Nurgle</strong> answered. 
          Nurgle — the Chaos God of plague, decay, and rot — 
          who experiences disease not as cruelty but as love. 
          In Nurgle's philosophy, all living things decay. 
          Decay is natural. Decay leads to new growth. 
          To be blessed with plague is to be loved, to be part of the cycle, 
          to never truly die.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Death Guard were saved. And they were remade. 
          Every Marine was transformed — bloated, rotting, diseased beyond recognition — 
          but unkillable for it. The same plague that had been killing them 
          became the source of their power. 
          They cannot die from disease because they <em>are</em> disease. 
          They trudge forward through bolter fire, plasma, and chainblades 
          with the serene patience of something that knows it cannot be killed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Mortarion became a Daemon Primarch — 
          one of the most powerful individual beings in the setting. 
          His wings spread a plague-storm wherever he walks. 
          And he is, in his own way, content. 
          This is what Nurgle wanted for him. 
          Father Nurgle loves all his children.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Playstyle -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-6">How they play on the table</h2>
      <div class="text-[#b8b4c0] leading-relaxed space-y-4 text-lg mb-10">
        <p>
          Death Guard are the most durable army in 40k. Full stop.
          Plague Marines have Toughness 5 (many weapons need a 5+ to wound them), 
          multiple wounds each, and <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Disgustingly Resilient</strong> — 
          a 5+ Feel No Pain save that lets them ignore wounds entirely on a roll of 5 or 6. 
          Killing a single Plague Marine requires dedication. Killing a squad of them 
          requires an entire army's worth of firepower. 
          Your opponent will be frustrated. This is the point.
        </p>
        <p>
          The army also has <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Contagion</strong> — 
          a spreading mechanic that reduces enemy Toughness the closer they get. 
          This means that as the game goes on and distances close, 
          your weapons become relatively more effective while your armor stays the same. 
          Time is on your side.
        </p>
        <p>
          The honest trade-off: <strong class="text-[#f0ede8]">Death Guard are slow</strong>. 
          Plague Marines lumber forward at a pace that gives opponents time to react, 
          reposition, and focus fire. You don't outmaneuver anyone. 
          You don't need to. You're the immovable object and you know it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The army is genuinely beginner-friendly — because you can make mistakes 
          and frequently survive them. Moved something slightly wrong? 
          It probably lives anyway. Got caught in a bad position? 
          You're still there next turn. 
          The durability buys you time to learn.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-green-400">Death Guard are for you if...</h3>
          <ul class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>✓ You want the most durable, unkillable army in the game</li>
            <li>✓ Rot, plague, and decay is your aesthetic</li>
            <li>✓ You want a forgiving army that survives your mistakes</li>
            <li>✓ You love technical paints, rust effects, and slime</li>
            <li>✓ You want a playstyle built on inevitability — slow, unstoppable advance</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-3 text-red-400">Maybe look elsewhere if...</h3>
          <ul class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm space-y-2">
            <li>✗ You want speed or mobility</li>
            <li>✗ You want a strong, versatile shooting army</li>
            <li>✗ You prefer clean, polished, non-gross aesthetics</li>
            <li>✗ You want psychic powers (Death Guard have very few)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Painting -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">How to paint Death Guard</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-4 text-lg">
        This is one of the most satisfying paint jobs in the entire game. 
        The techniques are simple, the results are spectacular, 
        and every "mistake" looks intentional.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] mb-10 leading-relaxed">
        Death Guard painting is a series of techniques that build on each other — 
        base, wash, rust, slime. Each step transforms the model further. 
        Games Workshop even makes specific technical paints for this faction 
        (Typhus Corrosion and Nurgle's Rot) that do most of the heavy lifting.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-10">
        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">1</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Spray Death Guard Green</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">GW makes a specific Death Guard Green spray primer — a pale, desaturated green-grey that's the perfect base for the diseased armor. If unavailable, Death World Forest is a close alternative.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">2</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Agrax Earthshade wash over the whole model</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Everything. The armor, the flesh, the weapons. One generous wash pools into every recess and turns the pale green immediately grimy and ancient. The model transforms completely in this step.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">3</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Drybrush Elysian Green on raised armor</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Restores a lighter green on the raised armor panels. Brings the model back from the darkness of the wash and adds definition to the armor shapes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">4</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Leadbelcher on all metal, then Typhus Corrosion stippled over it</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Leadbelcher on trim, bolters, and fittings first. Then stipple Typhus Corrosion (a chunky black-brown technical paint) over the metal — it creates an instant texture of corroded, pitted rust that looks incredible.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">5</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Drybrush Ryza Rust over the corroded metal</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">A bright orange rust drybrush over the Typhus Corrosion. The orange catches on the raised texture and creates convincing iron oxide rust — the most satisfying technique in the Death Guard toolkit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">6</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Pallid Wych Flesh on exposed bone, flesh, and maggots</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">Death Guard models have exposed flesh, tentacles, Nurgling infestations, and other organic protrusions. A pale flesh tone here — then a thin Carroburg Crimson wash in the recesses for raw, infected-looking skin.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex gap-4 bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] font-bold font-display flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 text-sm">7</div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm mb-1">Nurgle's Rot in wounds, vents, and open areas</p>
            <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm">The finishing touch. Nurgle's Rot is a technical paint that dries as a translucent green slime — pool it into bullet holes, wound openings, pipe vents, and mouth holes. It looks exactly like infectious ooze. One paint, used correctly, defines the entire faction.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold mb-2">Why this is so satisfying</h3>
        <p class="text-[#b8b4c0] text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Each step in the Death Guard recipe looks like a mistake until the next step fixes it. 
          The Agrax wash looks too dark. The rust looks too orange. 
          Then Nurgle's Rot goes on and suddenly the whole model reads as a coherent, 
          disgusting, incredibly detailed plague warrior. 
          New painters often produce their best work on Death Guard models 
          because the techniques do the work for you — 
          and every drip, smear, and blob looks like it belongs there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- What to buy -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-2">What to buy first</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] mb-10 text-lg">One box. The technical paints are half the hobby experience here — get them at the same time.</p>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#c9a84c] rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <div class="mb-4">
          <span class="bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113] text-xs font-semibold px-2 py-0.5 rounded font-display mr-2">Start Here</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-[#4a4855]">~$160</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-2">Combat Patrol: Death Guard</h3>
        <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Includes a Plague Marine Champion, Plague Marines (the elite core), 
          Poxwalkers (shambling plague-zombie troops — fast to paint, great for practicing techniques), 
          and a Foul Blightspawn (support character with a particularly revolting weapon). 
          The Poxwalkers alone are worth the purchase — 
          twenty grotesque figures that paint up shockingly fast.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.games-workshop.com/en-US/Death-Guard-Combat-Patrol"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded text-sm transition-colors font-display"
        >
          Buy from Games Workshop →
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <div class="mb-3">
          <span class="text-xs text-[#4a4855]">~$65 · the second purchase for most players</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-2">Plague Marines (7-model box)</h3>
        <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Your core unit, and some of the most detailed miniatures Games Workshop produces. 
          Every model is unique — no two poses are the same, and each one has its own 
          weapon, mutation, and personality. These are the models you'll spend the most time on, 
          and they're worth every minute.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://www.games-workshop.com/en-US/Plague-Marines"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded text-sm transition-colors font-display"
        >
          View on Games Workshop →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Paints -->
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-xl mb-4">Paints you need</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          {[
            { name: 'Death Guard Green Spray', use: 'Base coat — the defining armor color', required: true },
            { name: 'Agrax Earthshade', use: 'Full model wash — grime and shadow', required: true },
            { name: 'Elysian Green', use: 'Drybrush highlight on armor', required: true },
            { name: 'Leadbelcher', use: 'Metal trim and weapons', required: true },
            { name: 'Typhus Corrosion', use: 'Stipple on metal for rust texture', required: true },
            { name: 'Ryza Rust', use: 'Drybrush over corrosion for orange rust', required: true },
            { name: "Nurgle's Rot", use: 'The signature slime — wounds, vents, openings', required: true },
            { name: 'Pallid Wych Flesh', use: 'Exposed flesh, bone, and maggots', required: true },
            { name: 'Carroburg Crimson', use: 'Wash on flesh for infected-looking skin', required: false },
          ].map(paint => (
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class={`text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0 font-display ${paint.required ? 'bg-[#c9a84c] text-[#111113]' : 'border border-[#2a2a35] text-[#4a4855]'}`}>
                {paint.required ? 'Essential' : 'Optional'}
              </span>
              <div>
                <span class="text-sm font-semibold">{paint.name}</span>
                <span class="text-[#8a8590] text-sm"> — {paint.use}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-sm mt-4 italic">~$50 total — more paints than most factions, but Typhus Corrosion, Ryza Rust, and Nurgle's Rot are unique to this army and completely worth it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6"><div class="border-t border-[#2a2a35]"></div></div>

  <!-- Cost breakdown -->
  <section class="px-6 py-16 bg-[#0d0d0f]">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] mb-8">Honest cost breakdown</h2>
      <div class="bg-[#1a1a1f] border border-[#2a2a35] rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
        {[
          { item: 'Combat Patrol: Death Guard', cost: '~$160', note: 'Champion, Plague Marines, Poxwalkers, Blightspawn' },
          { item: 'Essential paints (8)', cost: '~$50', note: 'Includes 3 technical paints unique to Death Guard' },
          { item: 'Basing supplies', cost: '~$12', note: 'Brown mud texture + dead/scorched tufts' },
          { item: 'Clippers + hobby knife', cost: '~$20', note: 'One-time purchase' },
          { item: 'Superglue', cost: '~$5', note: 'Plague Marines have many small organic detail pieces' },
        ].map((row, i) => (
          <div class={`flex items-start justify-between px-5 py-4 gap-4 ${i % 2 === 0 ? '' : 'bg-[#111113]'}`}>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold">{row.item}</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[#4a4855] mt-0.5">{row.note}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[#c9a84c] flex-shrink-0">{row.cost}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 bg-[#1f1d14] border-t border-[#c9a84c]">
          <p class="font-display font-semibold">All-in starter total</p>
          <span class="font-display font-bold text-[#c9a84c] text-lg">~$247</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[#4a4855] text-sm mt-4 italic">
        The Plague Marines box (~$65) is the natural second purchase — your elite core, 
        and the models that showcase the rust and slime techniques best. 
        Mortarion (the Daemon Primarch) is the ultimate centerpiece if you ever want one — 
        but he's a large investment and a project for when you're confident with the techniques.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="px-6 py-20 text-center">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Father Nurgle loves you.</h2>
      <p class="text-[#8a8590] text-lg mb-8">
        Grab the Combat Patrol and the paints together — you'll want Typhus Corrosion 
        and Nurgle's Rot from day one. Paint the Poxwalkers first. 
        Then give a Plague Marine everything you've got.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a
          href="/painting"
          class="inline-block bg-[#c9a84c] hover:bg-[#dfc070] text-[#111113] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display tracking-wide"
        >
          Painting Guide →
        </a>
        <a
          href="/quiz"
          class="inline-block border border-[#2a2a35] hover:border-[#c9a84c] text-[#b8b4c0] hover:text-[#c9a84c] font-semibold px-8 py-4 rounded text-lg transition-colors font-display"
        >
          Retake the Quiz
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
